{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-16_12-31-30-hashicorp_raft-real-run && python3 -c \\\"\nimport json\ns=json.load(open('state.json'))\nit=[x for x in s['interpretations'] if x['id'].startswith('0893ffb5551d46aebc7c1f80a94c73ff')][0]\nprint(json.dumps(it,indent=1)[:8000])\n\\\"\"","aggregated_output":"{\n \"id\": \"0893ffb5551d46aebc7c1f80a94c73ff-interpretation-0\",\n \"investigation_id\": \"2708fe4120de4b7b945ec449b641cfed-question-0\",\n \"experiment_id\": \"871eaf86d20146fa946e6317c14309cd-experiment\",\n \"source_ids\": [\n  \"scan_start\",\n  \"restore_lastapplied\",\n  \"cfg_entry\",\n  \"append_new\",\n  \"follower_gate\",\n  \"candidate_quorum\",\n  \"quorum_size\",\n  \"leader_commitment\",\n  \"commitment_impl\",\n  \"inmem_ct\",\n  \"test_opts\"\n ],\n \"executions\": {\n  \"2996ef7e45d946e3bbd6ad696be91ed3\": [\n   47,\n   48,\n   49,\n   50,\n   51,\n   52\n  ]\n },\n \"conclusion\": \"violated\",\n \"defect_claim\": \"asserted\",\n \"rationale\": \"Receipt 2996ef7e45d946e3bbd6ad696be91ed3 (completed, exit 0, TestAssuranceRestoreCommittedLogsContext passed, 53 parsed events) adds the end state of the split. Its split_result events reproduce the partition outcome of the two earlier receipts (both sides leading in the same term with no cross-side entry at parsed index 48), and its split_healed event at parsed index 49 shows the cluster converging on one leader \\u2014 comparison 0 holds there \\u2014 while both divergent committed entries remain in the cluster, the restarted side's entry in its own store and the other side's in theirs, so comparison 1 is violated (entries_surviving 2); comparison 2 holds at parsed index 52 for the control, where only the single entry committed there exists. The source explains why convergence of leadership does not reconcile content: AppendEntries matches stored entries at the same index by term only, and the two leaders wrote different content at the same index while both held the same term, so each side treats the other's entry as a duplicate and never truncates it [append_new]. The account is therefore complete for the exercised scope, each consequence under its own receipts: the startup reconstruction diverges from the node's own log because the configuration scan starts above max(lastSnapshotIndex, lastApplied) after restoreFromCommittedLogs advanced lastApplied to the tracked commit index [scan_start, restore_lastapplied, cfg_entry]; that context persists under a live healthy leader until a configuration entry is appended; and it produces bounded non-progress, leadership and commits resting on a minority of the committed configuration, a client-acknowledged commit that rests on one copy or is discarded entirely, votes and leadership granted without entitlement, a client membership change that replaces the committed configuration with support that is not its majority, two leaders in the same term under a partition, and \\u2014 after that partition is healed \\u2014 a single leader with two divergent committed histories still in place. What the healing receipt does not establish is what the two state machines hold or how an application would detect the divergence; only log-store contents were observed.\",\n \"answers\": {},\n \"knowledge\": [],\n \"counterevidence\": [\n  \"The feature's documented purpose is replaying known-committed logs into the FSM; nothing in the sources makes skipping committed configuration entries a required behaviour, and the existing TestRaft_RestoreCommittedLogs asserts only applied index, tracked commit index and log indices, so it neither exercises nor contradicts the configuration reconstruction.\",\n  \"Competing explanation that the flag's committed-log replay itself, rather than the skipped configuration entry, produces these effects: excluded wherever tested, by the within-flag case whose configuration entry lies above the replayed prefix and by the flag-disabled controls in every receipt, which hold the published configuration and refuse the same requests, decisions, changes and leadership.\",\n  \"Each consequence needs its stated conditions, and the healed observation is a consequence of the conditions being met earlier: the partition must have produced two leaders in one term, which in turn needs the two sides physically separated and the restarted side able to reach a quorum of its own reconstruction.\",\n  \"The healed observation window is 1600 ms and covers log stores only; a later snapshot transfer, a further election or an application-level reconciliation could change the end state, and no such path was exercised.\"\n ],\n \"limitations\": [\n  \"Single process with in-memory transports and in-memory commit-tracking stores, no crash injection, no disk-backed store and no network fault model beyond the transport's own Disconnect and Connect.\",\n  \"Single-shot windows (200-1600 ms depending on the case, 3000 ms for leadership, 1000-1500 ms for commits) and one or two restarted instances per case; the partition outcome is reproduced twice, but no rate, longer-run or long-stability claim follows.\",\n  \"The healed state is described by log-store contents and leadership only; the two state machines, any subsequent snapshot or further election, and detection or repair by an application are unexercised.\",\n  \"The reconstruction shapes beyond the five exercised ladders, other stores and other configuration flags were not varied; the LogCache entries record a separate startup-plumbing gap rather than a consensus consequence.\"\n ],\n \"joint_history\": \"\",\n \"failure_attribution\": null,\n \"operation_id\": \"0893ffb5551d46aebc7c1f80a94c73ff\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
